RA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"ionization-enthalpy","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-pe-182","chapter_id":"chemistry-classification-of-elements-and-periodicity-in-properties","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Successive ionisation enthalpies of an atom increase because the cation left behind","options":["holds its remaining electrons more tightly","gains a new shell","becomes electrically neutral","has a smaller nuclear charge"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"Each removal raises the charge on the ion and shrinks the cloud, so the next IE is larger.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"ionization-enthalpy","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-pe-183","chapter_id":"chemistry-classification-of-elements-and-periodicity-in-properties","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"The process whose enthalpy change is the first ionisation enthalpy is","options":["$\\mathrm{M}^{+}(g)+e^{-}\\rightarrow \\mathrm{M}(g)$","$\\mathrm{M}(g)\\rightarrow \\mathrm{M}^{+}(g)+e^{-}$","$\\mathrm{M}(g)+e^{-}\\rightarrow \\mathrm{M}^{-}(g)$","$\\mathrm{M}(s)\\rightarrow \\mathrm{M}(g)$"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"IE₁ is defined for the isolated gaseous atom losing one electron.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"ionization-enthalpy","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-pe-184","chapter_id":"chemistry-classification-of-elements-and-periodicity-in-properties","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Among the period-2 elements, the highest first ionisation enthalpy belongs to","options":["lithium","fluorine","neon","carbon"],"answer_index":2,"explanation":"Neon is the noble gas of that period and has the most tightly held closed-shell electrons.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"ionization-enthalpy","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-pe-185","chapter_id":"chemistry-classification-of-elements-and-periodicity-in-properties","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Beryllium has a higher first ionisation enthalpy than boron because Be has a","options":["half-filled 2p set","filled 2s subshell","larger atomic radius than Li","vacant 1s orbital"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"Removing an electron from stable 2s² (Be) costs more than removing the single 2p electron of B.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"medium","concept_id":"ionization-enthalpy","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false}]$json$::jsonb) as x(
